      "text": "To find the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\), we need to determine \\(3^{12000} \\mod 1000\\). Given that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), we can use this information to simplify our calculation.\n\nFirst, we express \\(12000\\) in terms of \\(400\\):\n\\[12000 = 30 \\times 400.\\]\nThis means we can write:\n\\[3^{12000} = (3^{400})^{30}.\\]\nSince we know that \\(3^{400} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}\\), we can substitute this into the expression:\n\\[(3^{400})^{30} \\equiv 1^{30} \\pmod{1000}.\\]\nSince \\(1^{30} = 1\\), we have:\n\\[3^{12000} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}.\\]\nTherefore, the last three digits of \\(3^{12000}\\) are \\(\\boxed{001}\\).",
